<header><div class="header-inner">
<a href="#/index.html" class="logo"><span>IT</span>Code</a>
<nav><a href="#/index.html">Home</a><a href="/#programming">Programming</a><a href="/#networking">Networking</a><a href="/#other">Other</a></nav>
</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#programming">Programming</a> &rsaquo; APIs</div>
<article>
<h1>APIs</h1>
<p class="lede">An API is how one program talks to another. On the web that usually means sending HTTP requests and getting JSON back.</p>

<p>API stands for <em>Application Programming Interface</em> &mdash; a defined set of operations one program exposes for another to use. A web API is just that, delivered over HTTP: you send a request to a URL and get structured data (usually <a href="#/study/json.html">JSON</a>) back.</p>

<h2>REST, the common style</h2>
<p>Most web APIs follow a REST-ish convention: <strong>resources</strong> live at URLs, and HTTP methods act on them.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Request</th><th>Does</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>GET /users</code></td><td>List users</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>GET /users/42</code></td><td>Get one user</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>POST /users</code></td><td>Create a user</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>PATCH /users/42</code></td><td>Update a user</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>DELETE /users/42</code></td><td>Delete a user</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Calling one</h2>
<pre><code>const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/users/42", {
 headers: { "Authorization": "Bearer YOUR_TOKEN" }
});
const user = await res.json();
console.log(user.name);</code></pre>
<p>Always check <code>res.ok</code> (or the status code) before trusting the body &mdash; see the <a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP tutorial</a> for what the codes mean.</p>

<h2>Sending data</h2>
<pre><code>await fetch("https://api.example.com/users", {
 method: "POST",
 headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
 body: JSON.stringify({ name: "Ada" })
});</code></pre>

<h2>Authentication</h2>
<p>Most APIs need to know who you are. Common schemes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>API keys</strong> &mdash; a secret string, often in a header.</li>
<li><strong>Bearer tokens</strong> &mdash; <code>Authorization: Bearer ...</code>, frequently a JWT.</li>
<li><strong>OAuth</strong> &mdash; a flow that lets users grant limited access without sharing their password.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Good manners</h2>
<ul>
<li>Respect <strong>rate limits</strong> &mdash; a <code>429</code> means slow down.</li>
<li>Version your API (<code>/v1/...</code>) so you can change it without breaking callers.</li>
<li>Return useful status codes and error messages.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><strong>Never put secrets in front-end code.</strong> An API key shipped in browser JavaScript is visible to anyone. Keep it on a server and proxy the request.</div>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/python.html">Python</a><a href="#/study/javascript.html">JavaScript</a><a href="#/study/node.html">Node.js</a><a href="#/study/html.html">HTML &amp; CSS</a><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/dns.html">DNS</a></div>
</div>
</main>
