<header><div class="header-inner">
<a href="#/index.html" class="logo"><span>IT</span>Code</a>
<nav><a href="#/index.html">Home</a><a href="/#programming">Programming</a><a href="/#networking">Networking</a><a href="/#other">Other</a></nav>
</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#other">Other</a> &rsaquo; Caching</div>
<article>
<h1>Caching</h1>
<p class="lede">Caching is keeping a copy of something expensive so you do not have to recompute or refetch it. It is everywhere, and it is subtle.</p>

<p>A cache is a fast store of copies. Reading from it beats recomputing a result or crossing the network again. The idea is simple; getting it right &mdash; knowing when a copy is stale &mdash; is the famously hard part.</p>

<h2>Where caching happens on the web</h2>
<p>A single page load may be cached in several places at once:</p>
<ol>
<li>The <strong>browser</strong> caches files it already downloaded.</li>
<li>A <strong>CDN</strong> caches copies close to users geographically.</li>
<li>A <strong>reverse proxy</strong> caches responses in front of the app.</li>
<li>The <strong>application</strong> caches query results (often in Redis).</li>
<li>The <strong>database</strong> caches pages in memory.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Controlling the browser cache</h2>
<p>Servers steer caching with HTTP headers:</p>
<pre><code>Cache-Control: max-age=31536000, immutable # keep for a year
Cache-Control: no-cache # always revalidate first
Cache-Control: no-store # never cache at all
ETag: "a1b2c3" # a fingerprint of the content</code></pre>
<p>With an <code>ETag</code>, the browser can ask &ldquo;has it changed since <code>a1b2c3</code>?&rdquo; and the server can answer <code>304 Not Modified</code> &mdash; no body re-sent.</p>

<h2>Cache invalidation</h2>
<p>The hard problem: when the underlying thing changes, old copies must not linger. Common strategies:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>TTL</strong> &mdash; copies simply expire after a set time.</li>
<li><strong>Versioned URLs</strong> &mdash; include a content hash in the filename (<code>app.9f3a1.js</code>). Change the content, change the name; the old URL is never requested again. This is why you can cache such files for a year safely.</li>
<li><strong>Explicit purge</strong> &mdash; tell the CDN to drop a specific URL now.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><strong>The classic gotcha:</strong> &ldquo;it works for me but not for you&rdquo; is very often a stale cache somewhere in that stack &mdash; browser, CDN, or proxy &mdash; serving an old copy. A hard refresh or a versioned filename is the usual cure.</div>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/json.html">JSON</a><a href="#/study/sql.html">SQL</a><a href="#/study/databases.html">Databases</a><a href="#/study/linux.html">Linux</a><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/dns.html">DNS</a></div>
</div>
</main>
