<header><div class="header-inner">
<a href="#/index.html" class="logo"><span>IT</span>Code</a>
<nav><a href="#/index.html">Home</a><a href="/#programming">Programming</a><a href="/#networking">Networking</a><a href="/#other">Other</a></nav>
</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#other">Other</a> &rsaquo; Command Line</div>
<article>
<h1>Command Line</h1>
<p class="lede">The command line feels intimidating until a dozen commands click into place. These are the ones you will use every day.</p>

<p>The shell is a text interface to your computer. It is faster than clicking for a huge range of tasks, and it is often the <em>only</em> interface on a server. This covers the everyday toolkit.</p>

<h2>Getting around</h2>
<pre><code>pwd # where am I
ls -la # list files, including hidden, with detail
cd projects # change directory
cd .. # up one level
cd ~ # home</code></pre>

<h2>Working with files</h2>
<pre><code>cat file.txt # print a file
less file.txt # scroll through it (q to quit)
cp a.txt b.txt # copy
mv a.txt docs/ # move or rename
rm old.txt # delete
mkdir new-folder # make a directory</code></pre>

<h2>Finding things</h2>
<pre><code>grep "error" log.txt # lines containing "error"
grep -ri "todo" . # recursive, case-insensitive
find . -name "*.js" # files by name
find . -type f -mtime -1 # changed in the last day</code></pre>

<h2>Pipes: the superpower</h2>
<p>Each command does one thing; the pipe <code>|</code> feeds one command&rsquo;s output into the next. That composition is the whole philosophy of the shell:</p>
<pre><code># the 5 biggest files here
du -ah . | sort -rh | head -5

# how many lines mention "warning"
grep "warning" log.txt | wc -l</code></pre>

<h2>Redirection</h2>
<pre><code>command > out.txt # write output to a file (overwrite)
command >> out.txt # append
command 2> err.txt # redirect errors
command < in.txt # feed a file as input</code></pre>
<div class="note"><strong>Shortcuts that save time:</strong> Tab completes names, the up arrow recalls commands, <code>Ctrl+C</code> stops a running command, and <code>Ctrl+R</code> searches your history.</div>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/json.html">JSON</a><a href="#/study/sql.html">SQL</a><a href="#/study/databases.html">Databases</a><a href="#/study/linux.html">Linux</a><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/dns.html">DNS</a></div>
</div>
</main>
