<body>
<header><div class="header-inner">
<a href="#/index.html" class="logo"><span>IT</span>Code</a>
<nav><a href="#/index.html">Home</a><a href="/#programming">Programming</a><a href="/#networking">Networking</a><a href="/#other">Other</a></nav>
</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#other">Other</a> &rsaquo; Cookies</div>
<article>
<h1>Cookies</h1>
<p class="lede">HTTP forgets you the instant a request ends. Cookies are the small notes that let a site remember who you are.</p>

<p><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a> is stateless &mdash; each request stands alone, and the server has no built-in memory of the last one. <strong>Cookies</strong> fix that: the server hands the browser a small piece of data, and the browser sends it back on every subsequent request.</p>

<h2>How they are set</h2>
<p>The server includes a header in its response:</p>
<pre><code>Set-Cookie: session=abc123; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Max-Age=3600</code></pre>
<p>From then on the browser automatically attaches it:</p>
<pre><code>Cookie: session=abc123</code></pre>

<h2>The important attributes</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>HttpOnly</code></td><td>JavaScript cannot read it &mdash; blocks theft via <a href="#/study/security.html">XSS</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Secure</code></td><td>Only sent over HTTPS.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>SameSite</code></td><td>Controls whether it is sent on cross-site requests &mdash; the main <a href="#/study/security.html">CSRF</a> defense.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Max-Age</code> / <code>Expires</code></td><td>How long it lives. Without them it is a &ldquo;session&rdquo; cookie and vanishes when the browser closes.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Domain</code> / <code>Path</code></td><td>Which requests the cookie is attached to.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Sessions</h2>
<p>A login usually works like this: you sign in, the server creates a <strong>session</strong> and stores it, and gives you a cookie holding only an opaque session ID. The real data stays on the server; the cookie is just the claim ticket. Because it identifies you, a stolen session cookie is as good as your password &mdash; which is why <code>HttpOnly</code> and <code>Secure</code> matter.</p>

<h2>Cookies, localStorage, and tokens</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cookies</strong> &mdash; sent automatically with every request; good for sessions.</li>
<li><strong>localStorage</strong> &mdash; stays in the browser, never sent automatically; read by JavaScript.</li>
<li><strong>JWTs</strong> &mdash; signed tokens that can live in either, carrying claims the server can verify without a lookup.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><strong>Privacy note:</strong> &ldquo;third-party&rdquo; cookies &mdash; set by domains other than the one in the address bar &mdash; are what powered cross-site tracking, and browsers are busy phasing them out.</div>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/json.html">JSON</a><a href="#/study/sql.html">SQL</a><a href="#/study/databases.html">Databases</a><a href="#/study/linux.html">Linux</a><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/dns.html">DNS</a></div>
</div>
</main>
