<header><div class="header-inner">
<a href="#/index.html" class="logo"><span>IT</span>Code</a>
<nav><a href="#/index.html">Home</a><a href="/#programming">Programming</a><a href="/#networking">Networking</a><a href="/#other">Other</a></nav>
</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#other">Other</a> &rsaquo; Databases</div>
<article>
<h1>Databases</h1>
<p class="lede">A database is organized, durable storage you can query. The big choice is how structured your data is.</p>

<p>Any real application needs to keep data around between requests and restarts. A <strong>database</strong> is software built for exactly that: storing data durably and letting you query it efficiently.</p>

<h2>Relational (SQL) databases</h2>
<p>Data lives in tables with a fixed <strong>schema</strong> &mdash; defined columns and types. Tables relate to each other by keys, and you query with <a href="#/study/sql.html">SQL</a>. Examples: PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite.</p>
<p>They shine when:</p>
<ul>
<li>your data is structured and the shape is known,</li>
<li>relationships matter (users have orders, orders have items),</li>
<li>you need <strong>transactions</strong> &mdash; several changes that must all succeed or all fail.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Non-relational (NoSQL) databases</h2>
<p>A family of databases that drop the rigid table model for flexibility or scale:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Stores</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>Document</td><td>JSON-like documents</td><td>MongoDB</td></tr>
<tr><td>Key/value</td><td>Simple pairs, very fast</td><td>Redis</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wide-column</td><td>Huge, sparse tables</td><td>Cassandra</td></tr>
<tr><td>Graph</td><td>Nodes and relationships</td><td>Neo4j</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>ACID transactions</h2>
<p>The guarantee that makes relational databases trustworthy. A transaction is <strong>A</strong>tomic (all or nothing), <strong>C</strong>onsistent, <strong>I</strong>solated (concurrent transactions do not corrupt each other), and <strong>D</strong>urable (once committed, it survives a crash). Moving money between accounts is the classic case.</p>

<h2>Indexes</h2>
<p>An index is a lookup structure that makes queries on a column fast &mdash; the difference between scanning a million rows and jumping straight to the one you want. The trade-off: indexes take space and slow down writes slightly, so you add them where you actually query, not everywhere.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> start relational. It handles the vast majority of apps well, and you reach for NoSQL when you hit a specific need it solves &mdash; caching, massive scale, or genuinely schema-less data.</div>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/json.html">JSON</a><a href="#/study/sql.html">SQL</a><a href="#/study/linux.html">Linux</a><a href="#/study/cli.html">Command Line</a><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/dns.html">DNS</a></div>
</div>
</main>
