<header><div class="header-inner">
<a href="#/index.html" class="logo"><span>IT</span>Code</a>
<nav><a href="#/index.html">Home</a><a href="/#programming">Programming</a><a href="/#networking">Networking</a><a href="/#other">Other</a></nav>
</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#networking">Networking</a> &rsaquo; DNS</div>
<article>
<h1>DNS</h1>
<p class="lede">Computers talk in numbers, people remember names. DNS is the phone book that connects the two.</p>

<p>When you type <code>example.com</code>, your computer has no idea where that is. It needs an IP address like <code>93.184.216.34</code>. DNS (the Domain Name System) is the distributed database that does the lookup.</p>

<h2>The lookup, step by step</h2>
<ol>
<li>Your computer asks a <strong>resolver</strong> (usually run by your ISP, or something like <code>1.1.1.1</code>) for <code>example.com</code>.</li>
<li>If the resolver does not have it cached, it asks a <strong>root</strong> server, which replies: &ldquo;I do not know, but ask the <code>.com</code> servers.&rdquo;</li>
<li>The <code>.com</code> servers reply: &ldquo;ask the name servers for <code>example.com</code>.&rdquo;</li>
<li>Those <strong>authoritative</strong> name servers finally answer with the IP address.</li>
<li>The resolver caches the answer and hands it back to you.</li>
</ol>
<p>It looks like a lot of hops, but caching means most lookups are answered instantly from memory somewhere along the chain.</p>

<h2>Record types</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Record</th><th>Holds</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>A</code></td><td>An IPv4 address.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>AAAA</code></td><td>An IPv6 address.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>CNAME</code></td><td>An alias pointing at another name.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>MX</code></td><td>Where email for the domain should go.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>TXT</code></td><td>Arbitrary text (used for verification, SPF, DKIM).</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>NS</code></td><td>Which name servers are authoritative.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>TTL and caching</h2>
<p>Every record has a <strong>TTL</strong> (time to live) in seconds. It tells resolvers how long they may cache the answer. A low TTL (say 300) means changes propagate quickly but there are more lookups; a high TTL (86400) is efficient but a change can take a day to be seen everywhere.</p>

<h2>Looking it up yourself</h2>
<pre><code>$ dig example.com +short
93.184.216.34

$ dig example.com MX +short
0 .

$ nslookup example.com</code></pre>
<div class="note"><strong>Why a page can &ldquo;work for me but not for you&rdquo;:</strong> often it is a stale DNS cache. Changing a record and waiting out the old TTL &mdash; or flushing your resolver &mdash; fixes it.</div>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/tcp.html">TCP/IP</a><a href="#/study/tls.html">TLS / HTTPS</a><a href="#/study/websockets.html">WebSockets</a><a href="#/study/python.html">Python</a><a href="#/study/javascript.html">JavaScript</a></div>
</div>
</main>
