<body>
<header><div class="header-inner">
<a href="#/index.html" class="logo"><span>IT</span>Code</a>
<nav><a href="#/index.html">Home</a><a href="/#programming">Programming</a><a href="/#networking">Networking</a><a href="/#other">Other</a></nav>
</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#programming">Programming</a> &rsaquo; Git</div>
<article>
<h1>Git</h1>
<p class="lede">Git records the history of your project so you can experiment freely, undo mistakes, and work with others.</p>

<p>Git is a <strong>version control system</strong>. It snapshots your project over time, lets you branch off to try things, and merges work back together. Almost every software project uses it.</p>

<h2>The three areas</h2>
<p>A change moves through three places before it is shared:</p>
<ol>
<li>Your <strong>working directory</strong> &mdash; the files as they are right now.</li>
<li>The <strong>staging area</strong> &mdash; changes you have marked for the next commit (<code>git add</code>).</li>
<li>The <strong>repository</strong> &mdash; committed history (<code>git commit</code>).</li>
</ol>

<h2>Everyday commands</h2>
<pre><code>git status # what has changed
git add file.txt # stage a change
git add . # stage everything
git commit -m "message" # record a snapshot
git log --oneline # see history</code></pre>

<h2>Branches</h2>
<p>A branch is a movable pointer to a commit. Work on a feature without disturbing the main line:</p>
<pre><code>git switch -c new-feature # create and switch
# ...make commits...
git switch main
git merge new-feature # bring it back in</code></pre>

<h2>Working with a remote</h2>
<pre><code>git clone https://... repo # copy a repo
git pull # fetch + merge others' work
git push # send your commits up</code></pre>

<h2>Undoing things</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>You want to</th><th>Command</th></tr>
<tr><td>Unstage a file</td><td><code>git restore --staged file</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Discard a change</td><td><code>git restore file</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Fix the last commit message</td><td><code>git commit --amend</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Undo a commit, keep changes</td><td><code>git reset HEAD~1</code></td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note"><strong>Commit often, in small pieces.</strong> A tidy history of focused commits is far easier to read, review, and bisect than one giant commit at the end of the day.</div>
</article>
