<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#programming">Programming</a> &rsaquo; HTML &amp; CSS</div>
<article>
<h1>HTML &amp; CSS</h1>
<p class="lede">HTML says what things are; CSS says what they look like. Together they build every page you have ever seen.</p>

<p>A web page is two layers: <strong>HTML</strong> gives it structure and meaning, <strong>CSS</strong> gives it appearance. Keeping them separate is what makes pages maintainable.</p>

<h2>HTML: structure</h2>
<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
 &lt;head&gt;
 &lt;meta charset="utf-8"&gt;
 &lt;title&gt;My page&lt;/title&gt;
 &lt;/head&gt;
 &lt;body&gt;
 &lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;
 &lt;p&gt;A paragraph with a &lt;a href="/about"&gt;link&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
 &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>
<p>Elements nest inside each other, and many take <strong>attributes</strong> like <code>href</code> or <code>class</code>. Use elements for what they <em>mean</em> &mdash; <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;article&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> &mdash; not just how they look. That is &ldquo;semantic HTML,&rdquo; and it helps search engines and screen readers.</p>

<h2>CSS: style</h2>
<p>CSS is a list of rules. Each has a <strong>selector</strong> and a block of declarations:</p>
<pre><code>h1 {
 font-size: 32px;
 color: #2864d7;
}

.card {
 border: 1px solid #ddd;
 padding: 20px;
 border-radius: 8px;
}</code></pre>
<p>Selectors target elements by tag (<code>h1</code>), class (<code>.card</code>), or id (<code>#main</code>). When two rules conflict, the more <em>specific</em> one wins &mdash; this is &ldquo;specificity.&rdquo;</p>

<h2>The box model</h2>
<p>Every element is a box with <strong>content</strong>, <strong>padding</strong>, a <strong>border</strong>, and <strong>margin</strong> around it. One line saves a lot of surprises:</p>
<pre><code>* { box-sizing: border-box; }</code></pre>
<p>It makes <code>width</code> include padding and border, so sizes add up the way you expect.</p>

<h2>Layout</h2>
<p>Two modern tools do almost all layout:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Flexbox</strong> &mdash; arrange things in a row or column: <code>display: flex</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Grid</strong> &mdash; two-dimensional layouts: <code>display: grid</code>.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><strong>Responsive design:</strong> use <code>@media (max-width: 700px) { ... }</code> to change styles on small screens. Start simple and add breakpoints only where the layout actually breaks.</div>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/python.html">Python</a><a href="#/study/javascript.html">JavaScript</a><a href="#/study/node.html">Node.js</a><a href="#/study/git.html">Git</a><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/dns.html">DNS</a></div>
</div>
