<header><div class="header-inner">
<a href="#/index.html" class="logo"><span>IT</span>Code</a>
<nav><a href="#/index.html">Home</a><a href="/#programming">Programming</a><a href="/#networking">Networking</a><a href="/#other">Other</a></nav>
</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#networking">Networking</a> &rsaquo; HTTP</div>
<article>
<h1>HTTP</h1>
<p class="lede">Every page you open and every API you call speaks HTTP. Here is what actually travels between your browser and the server.</p>

<p>HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol) is a simple idea: the client sends a <strong>request</strong>, and the server sends back a <strong>response</strong>. Both are just text, followed optionally by a body. Once you have seen the raw shape of them, the rest of the web stops feeling like magic.</p>

<h2>A request</h2>
<p>When you visit a page, your browser sends something like this:</p>
<pre><code>GET /index.html HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com
User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0
Accept: text/html</code></pre>
<p>The first line has three parts: the <strong>method</strong> (<code>GET</code>), the <strong>path</strong> (<code>/index.html</code>), and the protocol version. Everything after it is <strong>headers</strong> &mdash; key/value pairs that describe the request.</p>

<h2>Methods</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Method</th><th>Means</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>GET</code></td><td>Fetch a resource. Should not change anything.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>POST</code></td><td>Send data to be processed (a form, a new record).</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>PUT</code></td><td>Replace a resource at a known location.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>PATCH</code></td><td>Partially update a resource.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>DELETE</code></td><td>Remove a resource.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>GET and HEAD are called <em>safe</em> methods because they only read. A browser will happily retry them; it is more careful with POST because it might do something twice.</p>

<h2>A response</h2>
<pre><code>HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8
Content-Length: 1270

&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;...</code></pre>
<p>The number <code>200</code> is the <strong>status code</strong>. The body comes after a blank line.</p>

<h2>Status codes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>2xx</strong> &mdash; success. <code>200 OK</code>, <code>201 Created</code>, <code>204 No Content</code>.</li>
<li><strong>3xx</strong> &mdash; redirection. <code>301</code> (moved permanently), <code>302</code>/<code>307</code> (temporary), <code>304 Not Modified</code> (use your cache).</li>
<li><strong>4xx</strong> &mdash; the client got it wrong. <code>400</code>, <code>401</code> (not authenticated), <code>403</code> (forbidden), <code>404</code> (not found), <code>429</code> (too many requests).</li>
<li><strong>5xx</strong> &mdash; the server got it wrong. <code>500</code>, <code>502</code> (bad gateway), <code>503</code> (unavailable).</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><strong>Tip:</strong> a <code>405 Method Not Allowed</code> means the URL exists but does not accept the method you used &mdash; for example, sending a <code>POST</code> to a page that only serves <code>GET</code>.</div>

<h2>Headers worth knowing</h2>
<ul>
<li><code>Content-Type</code> &mdash; what the body is (<code>text/html</code>, <code>application/json</code>, <code>image/png</code>).</li>
<li><code>Content-Length</code> &mdash; how many bytes the body is.</li>
<li><code>Cache-Control</code> &mdash; how long the response may be reused.</li>
<li><code>Authorization</code> &mdash; credentials for the request.</li>
<li><code>Location</code> &mdash; where a redirect points.</li>
</ul>
<p>HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 change how these bytes travel over the wire &mdash; multiplexing many requests over one connection, compressing headers &mdash; but the request/response model above stays exactly the same.</p>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
