<body>
<header><div class="header-inner">
<a href="#/index.html" class="logo"><span>IT</span>Code</a>
<nav><a href="#/index.html">Home</a><a href="/#programming">Programming</a><a href="/#networking">Networking</a><a href="/#other">Other</a></nav>
</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#programming">Programming</a> &rsaquo; JavaScript</div>
<article>
<h1>JavaScript</h1>
<p class="lede">JavaScript runs in every browser and makes pages interactive. Learn the language, then the DOM, then asynchronous code.</p>

<p>JavaScript is the one programming language every browser runs. It started small and grew into a full, modern language &mdash; and with Node.js it runs on servers too (see the <a href="#/study/node.html">Node.js tutorial</a>).</p>

<h2>Variables and types</h2>
<pre><code>const name = "Ada"; // cannot be reassigned
let count = 0; // can change
count = count + 1;

// types: string, number, boolean, null, undefined, object, symbol, bigint</code></pre>
<p>Prefer <code>const</code> by default and switch to <code>let</code> only when you actually reassign. Avoid the old <code>var</code>.</p>

<h2>Functions</h2>
<pre><code>function add(a, b) {
 return a + b;
}

// arrow function &mdash; shorter, and handy for callbacks
const double = (n) => n * 2;</code></pre>

<h2>Objects and arrays</h2>
<pre><code>const user = { id: 1, name: "Ada" };
user.name; // "Ada"

const nums = [3, 1, 2];
nums.map(n => n * 2); // [6, 2, 4]
nums.filter(n => n > 1);
nums.sort((a, b) => a - b);</code></pre>

<h2>Touching the page (the DOM)</h2>
<p>The <strong>DOM</strong> is the browser&rsquo;s object model of the page. JavaScript reads and changes it:</p>
<pre><code>const btn = document.querySelector("#save");
btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
 document.querySelector("#status").textContent = "Saved!";
});</code></pre>

<h2>Asynchronous code</h2>
<p>Network calls do not finish instantly, so JavaScript does not wait &mdash; it uses <strong>promises</strong>. <code>async</code>/<code>await</code> makes them read top-to-bottom:</p>
<pre><code>async function loadUser(id) {
 const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
 if (!res.ok) throw new Error("request failed");
 const data = await res.json();
 return data;
}</code></pre>
<div class="note"><strong>Mental model:</strong> JavaScript is single-threaded. Instead of blocking, it hands slow work (timers, network, clicks) to the browser and runs your callback later. That is the &ldquo;event loop.&rdquo;</div>
</article>
