</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#other">Other</a> &rsaquo; JSON</div>
<article>
<h1>JSON</h1>
<p class="lede">JSON is how programs hand structured data to each other. It is small, human-readable, and supported everywhere.</p>

<p>JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a text format for structured data. It grew out of JavaScript but is now language-neutral &mdash; every major language can read and write it, which is why APIs speak it.</p>

<h2>What it looks like</h2>
<pre><code>{
 "id": 42,
 "name": "Ada",
 "active": true,
 "roles": ["admin", "editor"],
 "profile": {
 "city": "London",
 "age": null
 }
}</code></pre>

<h2>The whole type system</h2>
<p>JSON has just six value types, and that is the point &mdash; it is deliberately tiny:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>string</strong> &mdash; always in double quotes: <code>"hello"</code></li>
<li><strong>number</strong> &mdash; <code>42</code>, <code>-3.14</code> (no distinction between int and float)</li>
<li><strong>boolean</strong> &mdash; <code>true</code> or <code>false</code></li>
<li><strong>null</strong></li>
<li><strong>array</strong> &mdash; ordered list in <code>[ ]</code></li>
<li><strong>object</strong> &mdash; key/value pairs in <code>{ }</code>; keys are always strings</li>
</ul>

<h2>Parsing and producing it</h2>
<pre><code>// JavaScript
const obj = JSON.parse(text); // text -> value
const text = JSON.stringify(obj); // value -> text
JSON.stringify(obj, null, 2); // pretty-printed</code></pre>
<pre><code># Python
import json
obj = json.loads(text)
text = json.dumps(obj, indent=2)</code></pre>

<h2>Rules that trip people up</h2>
<ul>
<li>Keys and strings must use <strong>double</strong> quotes, never single.</li>
<li>No trailing commas.</li>
<li>No comments &mdash; JSON has none.</li>
<li>No <code>undefined</code>, no functions, no dates &mdash; dates are usually sent as ISO strings like <code>"2026-11-08T12:00:00Z"</code>.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><strong>Related:</strong> YAML and TOML are friendlier for config files (they allow comments), but for machine-to-machine data over the wire, JSON is the default.</div>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/sql.html">SQL</a><a href="#/study/databases.html">Databases</a><a href="#/study/linux.html">Linux</a><a href="#/study/cli.html">Command Line</a><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/dns.html">DNS</a></div>
</div>
</main>
