<header><div class="header-inner">
<a href="#/index.html" class="logo"><span>IT</span>Code</a>
<nav><a href="#/index.html">Home</a><a href="/#programming">Programming</a><a href="/#networking">Networking</a><a href="/#other">Other</a></nav>
</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#other">Other</a> &rsaquo; Linux</div>
<article>
<h1>Linux</h1>
<p class="lede">Most servers run Linux. Knowing your way around the filesystem, permissions, and processes pays off constantly.</p>

<p>Linux is a free, Unix-like operating system that powers the majority of the world&rsquo;s servers. You interact with it mostly through the <a href="#/study/cli.html">command line</a>, so a little fluency goes a long way.</p>

<h2>The filesystem</h2>
<p>Everything hangs off a single root, <code>/</code>. Some directories you will meet:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Path</th><th>Holds</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>/home</code></td><td>Users&rsquo; personal files</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>/etc</code></td><td>System configuration</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>/var</code></td><td>Logs, spool, changing data</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>/usr</code></td><td>Installed programs and libraries</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>/tmp</code></td><td>Temporary files</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Permissions</h2>
<p>Every file has an owner, a group, and three sets of permissions &mdash; read, write, execute &mdash; for owner, group, and everyone else:</p>
<pre><code>$ ls -l script.sh
-rwxr-xr-- 1 ada staff 128 Nov 8 10:00 script.sh

$ chmod +x script.sh # make executable
$ chmod 644 notes.txt # rw-r--r--
$ chown ada notes.txt # change owner</code></pre>
<p>The digits are octal: read=4, write=2, execute=1, added up per group. So <code>644</code> is read/write for the owner, read-only for everyone else.</p>

<h2>Processes</h2>
<pre><code>$ ps aux # list running processes
$ top # live view of CPU/memory
$ kill 1234 # ask process 1234 to stop
$ kill -9 1234 # force it
$ systemctl status caddy # a managed service</code></pre>

<h2>The superuser</h2>
<p><code>root</code> can do anything. You usually run a normal account and prefix privileged commands with <code>sudo</code>:</p>
<pre><code>$ sudo systemctl restart nginx</code></pre>
<div class="note"><strong>Be deliberate with <code>sudo</code> and <code>rm -rf</code>.</strong> There is no recycle bin on the command line &mdash; a deleted file is gone. Double-check the path before you press enter.</div>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/json.html">JSON</a><a href="#/study/sql.html">SQL</a><a href="#/study/databases.html">Databases</a><a href="#/study/cli.html">Command Line</a><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/dns.html">DNS</a></div>
