<header><div class="header-inner">
<a href="#/index.html" class="logo"><span>IT</span>Code</a>
<nav><a href="#/index.html">Home</a><a href="/#programming">Programming</a><a href="/#networking">Networking</a><a href="/#other">Other</a></nav>
</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#programming">Programming</a> &rsaquo; Node.js</div>
<article>
<h1>Node.js</h1>
<p class="lede">Node.js takes JavaScript out of the browser and onto the server &mdash; where it reads files, talks to databases, and serves HTTP.</p>

<p>Node.js is JavaScript with the browser swapped out for the operating system. The same language you use for page scripts now reads files, opens network sockets, and runs servers.</p>

<h2>Running code</h2>
<pre><code>$ node app.js # run a file
$ node # interactive REPL</code></pre>

<h2>Modules</h2>
<p>Code is split into modules. Modern Node uses ES modules:</p>
<pre><code>// math.js
export function add(a, b) { return a + b; }

// app.js
import { add } from "./math.js";
console.log(add(2, 3));</code></pre>
<p>You will also see the older CommonJS style (<code>require()</code> and <code>module.exports</code>) in lots of existing code.</p>

<h2>npm</h2>
<p><strong>npm</strong> is the package manager. <code>package.json</code> records your project&rsquo;s dependencies:</p>
<pre><code>$ npm init -y # create package.json
$ npm install express # add a dependency
$ npm run start # run a script you defined</code></pre>

<h2>A minimal HTTP server</h2>
<pre><code>import { createServer } from "node:http";

const server = createServer((req, res) => {
 res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "text/plain" });
 res.end("Hello from Node
");
});

server.listen(3000, () => console.log("listening on :3000"));</code></pre>
<p>In real projects you would usually reach for a framework like <strong>Express</strong> or <strong>Fastify</strong> to handle routing and parsing for you.</p>

<h2>Why it is popular for servers</h2>
<ul>
<li>One language across front end and back end.</li>
<li>Non-blocking I/O &mdash; it handles many connections at once without a thread per request.</li>
<li>A huge ecosystem of packages on npm.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><strong>Watch out:</strong> because Node runs your callbacks on a single main thread, a heavy synchronous loop blocks <em>everyone</em>. Keep CPU-heavy work off the main thread or push it to a worker.</div>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/python.html">Python</a><a href="#/study/javascript.html">JavaScript</a><a href="#/study/html.html">HTML &amp; CSS</a><a href="#/study/git.html">Git</a><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/dns.html">DNS</a></div>
</div>
</main>
