<header><div class="header-inner">
<a href="#/index.html" class="logo"><span>IT</span>Code</a>
<nav><a href="#/index.html">Home</a><a href="/#programming">Programming</a><a href="/#networking">Networking</a><a href="/#other">Other</a></nav>
</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#other">Other</a> &rsaquo; Web Security</div>
<article>
<h1>Web Security</h1>
<p class="lede">You do not need to be a security researcher, but you do need to recognize the handful of attacks that hit web apps constantly.</p>

<p>Most breaches are not exotic &mdash; they exploit the same few mistakes again and again. Knowing them is most of the defense.</p>

<h2>Injection (including SQL injection)</h2>
<p>If you paste user input straight into a command or query, the user can change what it does:</p>
<pre><code># DANGEROUS &mdash; user input glued into SQL
"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '" + input + "'"</code></pre>
<p>Input of <code>' OR '1'='1</code> turns that into a query matching everyone. <strong>Fix:</strong> always use <strong>parameterized queries</strong> &mdash; the database treats input as data, never as code.</p>

<h2>Cross-site scripting (XSS)</h2>
<p>If you render user content as raw HTML, an attacker can inject <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> that runs in your other users&rsquo; browsers &mdash; stealing sessions or defacing the page. <strong>Fix:</strong> escape output by default, and set a <strong>Content-Security-Policy</strong> header to restrict what scripts can run.</p>

<h2>Cross-site request forgery (CSRF)</h2>
<p>A malicious page makes the victim&rsquo;s browser send a request to your site using their logged-in <a href="#/study/cookies.html">cookies</a>. <strong>Fix:</strong> require a secret <strong>CSRF token</strong> on state-changing requests, and use <code>SameSite</code> cookies.</p>

<h2>Authentication mistakes</h2>
<ul>
<li>Never store passwords in plain text &mdash; hash them with <strong>bcrypt</strong>, <strong>scrypt</strong>, or <strong>argon2</strong>.</li>
<li>Put every page behind <a href="#/study/tls.html">HTTPS</a> so credentials and cookies are never sent in the clear.</li>
<li>Offer multi-factor authentication for anything sensitive.</li>
</ul>

<h2>A short checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>Validate and escape all input and output.</li>
<li>Use parameterized queries everywhere.</li>
<li>Keep dependencies patched &mdash; most vulnerabilities come from old libraries.</li>
<li>Give accounts and services the <strong>least privilege</strong> they need.</li>
<li>Do not leak detail in error messages.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><strong>Golden rule:</strong> never trust input from the client. Validate on the server no matter what the front end already checked &mdash; the front end can be bypassed entirely.</div>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/json.html">JSON</a><a href="#/study/sql.html">SQL</a><a href="#/study/databases.html">Databases</a><a href="#/study/linux.html">Linux</a><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/dns.html">DNS</a></div>
</div>
</main>
