<header><div class="header-inner">
<a href="#/index.html" class="logo"><span>IT</span>Code</a>
<nav><a href="#/index.html">Home</a><a href="/#programming">Programming</a><a href="/#networking">Networking</a><a href="/#other">Other</a></nav>
</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#other">Other</a> &rsaquo; Web Servers</div>
<article>
<h1>Web Servers</h1>
<p class="lede">A web server answers HTTP requests. Modern setups layer a reverse proxy in front of your application to handle the hard parts.</p>

<p>A <strong>web server</strong> is a program that listens for <a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a> requests and sends back responses &mdash; files, or output from your application. Common ones are Nginx, Apache, Caddy, and the dev servers built into frameworks.</p>

<h2>Serving static files</h2>
<p>The simplest job: map a URL to a file on disk and send it. A request for <code>/about.html</code> returns the file at <code>/var/www/about.html</code>. Fast, cacheable, and plenty for a static site.</p>

<h2>The reverse proxy</h2>
<p>For dynamic apps, you usually do <em>not</em> expose your application directly. Instead a web server sits in front as a <strong>reverse proxy</strong>, forwarding requests to your app running on an internal port:</p>
<pre><code>Browser -> Caddy/Nginx (:443) -> your app (:3000)</code></pre>
<p>The proxy handles the things your app should not have to:</p>
<ul>
<li>terminating <a href="#/study/tls.html">TLS</a> (certificates, HTTPS),</li>
<li>serving static files and <a href="#/study/caching.html">caching</a>,</li>
<li>compression, rate limiting, and logging,</li>
<li>routing different paths or hostnames to different apps.</li>
</ul>

<h2>A tiny Caddy config</h2>
<pre><code>example.com {
 root * /var/www
 file_server
 reverse_proxy /api/* localhost:3000
}</code></pre>
<p>That serves files from a folder, proxies anything under <code>/api/</code> to a local app, and &mdash; because it is Caddy &mdash; obtains and renews an HTTPS certificate automatically.</p>

<h2>Load balancing</h2>
<p>When one app instance is not enough, a <strong>load balancer</strong> (often the same reverse proxy) spreads requests across several, and stops sending traffic to any that fall over. That is the foundation of scaling a web service horizontally.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Forwarded headers:</strong> behind a proxy your app sees the proxy&rsquo;s IP, not the visitor&rsquo;s. The proxy adds <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> so the real client IP is still available &mdash; make sure your app reads it.</div>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/json.html">JSON</a><a href="#/study/sql.html">SQL</a><a href="#/study/databases.html">Databases</a><a href="#/study/linux.html">Linux</a><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/dns.html">DNS</a></div>
</div>
</main>
