<header><div class="header-inner">
<a href="#/index.html" class="logo"><span>IT</span>Code</a>
<nav><a href="#/index.html">Home</a><a href="/#programming">Programming</a><a href="/#networking">Networking</a><a href="/#other">Other</a></nav>
</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#other">Other</a> &rsaquo; SQL</div>
<article>
<h1>SQL</h1>
<p class="lede">SQL is the language for asking questions of a relational database. A handful of keywords covers most of what you will ever write.</p>

<p>SQL (Structured Query Language) is how you read and change data in relational databases like PostgreSQL, MySQL, and SQLite. Data lives in <strong>tables</strong> &mdash; rows and columns &mdash; and SQL describes <em>what</em> you want, not how to fetch it.</p>

<h2>Reading data</h2>
<pre><code>SELECT name, email
FROM users
WHERE active = true
ORDER BY name
LIMIT 10;</code></pre>
<p>Read it as: pick these columns, from this table, keep rows matching this condition, sort them, and stop after ten.</p>

<h2>Filtering</h2>
<pre><code>WHERE age >= 18
WHERE name LIKE 'A%' -- starts with A
WHERE country IN ('US', 'UK')
WHERE deleted_at IS NULL</code></pre>

<h2>Aggregating</h2>
<pre><code>SELECT country, COUNT(*) AS people
FROM users
GROUP BY country
HAVING COUNT(*) > 100;</code></pre>
<p><code>GROUP BY</code> collapses rows into groups; aggregate functions like <code>COUNT</code>, <code>SUM</code>, <code>AVG</code> summarize each group. <code>HAVING</code> filters the groups (<code>WHERE</code> filters rows before grouping).</p>

<h2>Joining tables</h2>
<p>Relational data is split across tables and reconnected with <strong>joins</strong>:</p>
<pre><code>SELECT orders.id, users.name
FROM orders
JOIN users ON users.id = orders.user_id;</code></pre>
<p>An <code>INNER JOIN</code> keeps only matching rows; a <code>LEFT JOIN</code> keeps every row from the left table even when there is no match.</p>

<h2>Changing data</h2>
<pre><code>INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES ('Ada', 'ada@x.com');
UPDATE users SET active = false WHERE id = 42;
DELETE FROM users WHERE id = 42;</code></pre>
<div class="note"><strong>Danger:</strong> <code>UPDATE</code> and <code>DELETE</code> without a <code>WHERE</code> clause hit <em>every</em> row. Run a <code>SELECT</code> with the same condition first to see what you are about to touch.</div>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/json.html">JSON</a><a href="#/study/databases.html">Databases</a><a href="#/study/linux.html">Linux</a><a href="#/study/cli.html">Command Line</a><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/dns.html">DNS</a></div>
</div>
</main>
