</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#other">Other</a> &rsaquo; SSH</div>
<article>
<h1>SSH</h1>
<p class="lede">SSH is the standard way to get a secure shell on a remote machine. Learn the command, then switch to keys.</p>

<p>SSH (Secure Shell) gives you an encrypted connection to another computer&rsquo;s command line. It is how almost everyone administers servers. The traffic is encrypted end to end, so it is safe even over untrusted networks.</p>

<h2>Connecting</h2>
<pre><code>ssh ada@203.0.113.10
ssh ada@server.example.com -p 2222 # non-default port</code></pre>
<p>The first time, SSH shows the server&rsquo;s <strong>fingerprint</strong> and asks you to confirm. It remembers it in <code>~/.ssh/known_hosts</code>, and warns you loudly if it ever changes &mdash; which could mean the server was rebuilt, or something is impersonating it.</p>

<h2>Keys instead of passwords</h2>
<p>Password logins are brute-forceable. A <strong>key pair</strong> is far stronger and more convenient. You generate two linked keys &mdash; a private one you keep, a public one you hand out:</p>
<pre><code>$ ssh-keygen -t ed25519
# creates ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 (private) and id_ed25519.pub (public)

$ ssh-copy-id ada@server # install your public key there</code></pre>
<p>Now you log in with no password, and the private key never leaves your machine. Protect it with a passphrase, and let <code>ssh-agent</code> hold it unlocked during your session.</p>

<h2>Copying files</h2>
<pre><code>scp file.txt ada@server:/home/ada/ # copy up
scp ada@server:/var/log/app.log . # copy down
rsync -av ./site/ ada@server:/var/www/ # sync a folder</code></pre>

<h2>Config shortcuts</h2>
<p>Put common hosts in <code>~/.ssh/config</code> so you can type <code>ssh web</code> instead of the full address:</p>
<pre><code>Host web
 HostName 203.0.113.10
 User ada
 Port 2222</code></pre>
<div class="note"><strong>Hardening a server:</strong> once key login works, disable password authentication entirely in <code>/etc/ssh/sshd_config</code> (<code>PasswordAuthentication no</code>) to shut the door on brute-force attempts.</div>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/json.html">JSON</a><a href="#/study/sql.html">SQL</a><a href="#/study/databases.html">Databases</a><a href="#/study/linux.html">Linux</a><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/dns.html">DNS</a></div>
</div>
</main>
