<header><div class="header-inner">
<a href="#/index.html" class="logo"><span>IT</span>Code</a>
<nav><a href="#/index.html">Home</a><a href="/#programming">Programming</a><a href="/#networking">Networking</a><a href="/#other">Other</a></nav>
</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#networking">Networking</a> &rsaquo; TCP/IP</div>
<article>
<h1>TCP/IP</h1>
<p class="lede">Underneath HTTP and almost everything else sits TCP/IP &mdash; the pair of protocols that move bytes across the internet without losing them.</p>

<p>The internet is a <em>packet-switched</em> network: data is chopped into small packets, each routed independently, and reassembled at the far end. Two protocols do most of the work &mdash; <strong>IP</strong> handles addressing and routing, <strong>TCP</strong> handles reliability.</p>

<h2>IP: addressing</h2>
<p>Every device has an IP address. IPv4 looks like <code>192.168.1.10</code> (four bytes). IPv6 looks like <code>2001:db8::1</code> and exists because we ran out of IPv4 addresses. IP gets a packet <em>toward</em> a destination by hopping through routers, but it makes no promises &mdash; packets can arrive out of order, duplicated, or not at all.</p>

<h2>TCP: reliability</h2>
<p>TCP builds a dependable stream on top of unreliable IP. It:</p>
<ul>
<li>numbers every byte so the other side can reorder them,</li>
<li>acknowledges what it received, and retransmits what went missing,</li>
<li>uses <strong>flow control</strong> and <strong>congestion control</strong> so it does not overwhelm the receiver or the network.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The three-way handshake</h2>
<p>Before any data flows, TCP sets up a connection:</p>
<pre><code>Client --- SYN ---> Server
Client <-- SYN/ACK -- Server
Client --- ACK ---> Server
 (connection open)</code></pre>
<p>This is why a brand-new connection has a little latency cost &mdash; a full round trip before the first byte of real data. (TLS then adds its own handshake on top; see the <a href="#/study/tls.html">TLS tutorial</a>.)</p>

<h2>Ports</h2>
<p>An IP address finds the machine; a <strong>port</strong> finds the program on it. Some well-known ports:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Port</th><th>Service</th></tr>
<tr><td>80</td><td>HTTP</td></tr>
<tr><td>443</td><td>HTTPS</td></tr>
<tr><td>22</td><td>SSH</td></tr>
<tr><td>53</td><td>DNS</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>TCP vs UDP</h2>
<p>UDP is the no-promises alternative: fire a packet and hope. It has no handshake and no retransmission, which makes it fast and great for video calls, games, and DNS, where a slightly late packet is worse than a lost one. HTTP/3 actually runs over UDP (via QUIC) and rebuilds reliability itself.</p>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/dns.html">DNS</a><a href="#/study/tls.html">TLS / HTTPS</a><a href="#/study/websockets.html">WebSockets</a><a href="#/study/python.html">Python</a><a href="#/study/javascript.html">JavaScript</a></div>
</div>
</main>
