<header><div class="header-inner">
<a href="#/index.html" class="logo"><span>IT</span>Code</a>
<nav><a href="#/index.html">Home</a><a href="/#programming">Programming</a><a href="/#networking">Networking</a><a href="/#other">Other</a></nav>
</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#networking">Networking</a> &rsaquo; TLS / HTTPS</div>
<article>
<h1>TLS / HTTPS</h1>
<p class="lede">HTTPS is just HTTP inside an encrypted TLS tunnel. Here is how that tunnel is built and what it guarantees.</p>

<p>Plain HTTP travels in the clear &mdash; anyone between you and the server can read or change it. <strong>TLS</strong> (Transport Layer Security, the successor to SSL) wraps the connection so that it is private, tamper-evident, and authenticated. HTTP over TLS is what we call HTTPS.</p>

<h2>What TLS gives you</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Confidentiality</strong> &mdash; the data is encrypted, so eavesdroppers see only noise.</li>
<li><strong>Integrity</strong> &mdash; if someone flips a bit in transit, it is detected.</li>
<li><strong>Authentication</strong> &mdash; a certificate proves you are really talking to the site you think you are.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The handshake</h2>
<p>After TCP connects, TLS negotiates the secure channel:</p>
<ol>
<li>The client says hello and lists the cipher suites and TLS versions it supports.</li>
<li>The server picks one and sends its <strong>certificate</strong>.</li>
<li>They perform a key exchange (modern TLS uses ephemeral Diffie&ndash;Hellman) to agree on a shared secret that no eavesdropper can derive.</li>
<li>Both switch to symmetric encryption using that secret, and the real HTTP begins.</li>
</ol>
<p>TLS 1.3 streamlined this to a single round trip, and can even resume a previous session with zero round trips.</p>

<h2>Certificates and trust</h2>
<p>A certificate binds a public key to a domain name, and is signed by a <strong>Certificate Authority</strong> (CA) that your browser already trusts. The chain looks like: your browser trusts a root CA &rarr; the root signs an intermediate &rarr; the intermediate signs the site&rsquo;s certificate. If any link is broken or expired, you get a warning.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>The padlock does not mean &ldquo;safe.&rdquo;</strong> It means the connection is encrypted and the certificate matches the domain. A phishing site can have a perfectly valid certificate for its own look-alike domain.</div>

<h2>Getting a certificate</h2>
<p>These days certificates are free and automatic. Tools like <strong>Let&rsquo;s Encrypt</strong> issue them after proving you control the domain, and servers like Caddy renew them on their own. You rarely touch a <code>.pem</code> file by hand anymore.</p>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/dns.html">DNS</a><a href="#/study/tcp.html">TCP/IP</a><a href="#/study/websockets.html">WebSockets</a><a href="#/study/python.html">Python</a><a href="#/study/javascript.html">JavaScript</a></div>
</div>
</main>
