</div></header>
<main>
<div class="crumb"><a href="#/index.html">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/#networking">Networking</a> &rsaquo; WebSockets</div>
<article>
<h1>WebSockets</h1>
<p class="lede">HTTP is a one-shot request and reply. WebSockets keep the line open so the server can talk back whenever it wants.</p>

<p>Normal HTTP is a monologue with a reply: you ask, the server answers, the connection is done. That is fine for loading a page, but awful for a chat app or a live scoreboard, where the <em>server</em> needs to push updates to <em>you</em>. WebSockets solve exactly that.</p>

<h2>The upgrade</h2>
<p>A WebSocket starts life as an ordinary HTTP request with a special header asking to switch protocols:</p>
<pre><code>GET /chat HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com
Upgrade: websocket
Connection: Upgrade
Sec-WebSocket-Key: dGhlIHNhbXBsZSBub25jZQ==</code></pre>
<p>If the server agrees, it replies <code>101 Switching Protocols</code>, and from that point the same TCP connection is a full-duplex WebSocket &mdash; both sides can send <strong>frames</strong> at any time, with almost no per-message overhead.</p>

<h2>Using one in the browser</h2>
<pre><code>const ws = new WebSocket("wss://example.com/chat");

ws.onopen = () => ws.send("hello");
ws.onmessage = (e) => console.log("got:", e.data);
ws.onclose = () => console.log("closed");</code></pre>
<p>Note the <code>wss://</code> scheme &mdash; that is WebSocket over TLS, the encrypted version, just like <code>https</code> is to <code>http</code>.</p>

<h2>When to reach for them</h2>
<ul>
<li>Chat and messaging.</li>
<li>Live dashboards, prices, sports scores.</li>
<li>Multiplayer games and collaborative editors.</li>
<li>Anything where waiting for the client to ask again would feel laggy.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Alternatives</h2>
<p>If you only need the server to push <em>to</em> the client (not both ways), <strong>Server-Sent Events</strong> are simpler and run over plain HTTP. For request/response with occasional pushes, long-polling still works. WebSockets win when traffic is genuinely two-way and frequent.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Heads up:</strong> a WebSocket that is idle for a while can be dropped by proxies or load balancers. Apps usually send a small <em>ping</em> frame every so often to keep it alive.</div>
</article>
<div class="more">
<h2>Keep reading</h2>
<div class="more-grid"><a href="#/study/http.html">HTTP</a><a href="#/study/dns.html">DNS</a><a href="#/study/tcp.html">TCP/IP</a><a href="#/study/tls.html">TLS / HTTPS</a><a href="#/study/python.html">Python</a><a href="#/study/javascript.html">JavaScript</a></div>
</div>
</main>
